{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-316692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=2-{y}^{8}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-{y}^{8})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1}\\,-\\,{\\frac{{y}^{9}}{9}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1-0)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{9}-0) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-316692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=24-6{y}^{2}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-3$ and $y=-2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(24-6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,6{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24y\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2}-2{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =24(-2-(-3))-2(-8-(-27)) \\\\  & =24-38 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-14|=14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-346471" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346471" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/1840ce2886aa3546371998239757a5dc500449007dc511dc0860b1ce59a59d2c.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+2y,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}+2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)+2(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-11186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "\\frac{22}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{350}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{44}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{68}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{44}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/b4408c4dc92d7145fef94603269f2cc54ceeb3bb38703401ff46004a2428dd3f.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x=-{y}^{5}+1,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{y}^{5}+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1}+y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0)+(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-11216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{19}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-11216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-10025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area bounded between the curve $x=(y-1)^{2},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-10025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{1}{2}-0)+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-10025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-10025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-10025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-10025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/5e3f9d31bc4cb5687d784097679861644cc55ce667ea1469d0d790bfe879706f.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4(2-0)-(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-78892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{16}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/7808295803d04d2c996ca7d36110f070a60f4491107ad1c831e6fa8ea9f85678.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi y) & =1 \\\\ \\pi y & =…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},… \\\\ y & =…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{3}{4}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4}-{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-346702" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346702" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-117485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-117485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(9-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/bbcf218e7b6181c861ba4c804357cc53ae6004453914259a9fe98ad5f09ac4d4.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0)-(\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}-\\frac{81}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-117485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-117485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-117485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "27"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{81}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{81}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-117485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{81}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/c5cd702bed84fd0ffc7f0e3a9510bdb3411bb8f77424786f9445c4d559392416.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-4$ to $y=4.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-6y\\,{|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(64-(-64))-6(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =16-48 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is $|-32|=32.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-346643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=2{y}^{2}-2$ and the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/a2156d559a715d668f064c91132719d2678ab4af66bf83591fa5687d6e23d95e.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=2{y}^{2}-2$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-4\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-4(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{8}{3}|=\\frac{8}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-78886" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78886" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78886" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={e}^{y}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(e-1)-(1-0) \\\\  & =e-1-1 \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-346744" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-346744" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/446dddc9ec5393005b5aa9fff9d45760c6f2e80da97fd459cdd74cf4f8575f71.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}+1,$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x-1}+1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\\\ (y-1)^{3} & =x-1 \\\\ x & =(y-1)^{3}+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+3{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}+3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+3(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-1+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-78896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/c730bd61b118d5c0418274498fe9112b9a6affd2c9a8699f612978693e0a4459.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{4x-8},$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{4x-8} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =4x-8 \\\\ {y}^{3}+8 & =4x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{3}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{-1}^{2}+2\\cdot y{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(16-1)+2(2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{16}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{111}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-78899" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78899" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{111}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{231}{32}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{37}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{57}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78899" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{111}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}} ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{y}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-316744" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-316744" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e^2-1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2-{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{0}-{e}^{y}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =2(0+2)-({e}^{0}-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4-(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c6c7aa8b5a2f5b3f54cef964c69ccb34d192b68ea407470d7d558e8ecba9de6"} {"question" "q-78900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "1+e"] ["math" "3+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "3+{e}^{2}"] ["math" "5+e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "q-78900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5a5b3f521fcc91c260d102b43ac6f089b7e3b6c6c852ffe330030809c3ffe64"} {"question" "e-425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/bf61fd6b36dd17d2c9f6d6f570a8bad9111d10da11df8f69e39069d7fb4478cf.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/8cdef67ff9afa667ed298cc0bb29e1727cfd7208fca347fb2b0ae253a1abdebf.png) the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/0389cce819c15d700c69e6ade974bd826bb0524b355de895a27e69589ad0afd5.png)-axis, and the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/81356f9909beb212c6d1c0dd7d3792dfacae911b236f1c8fa22ff0d3a508544e.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/32dbbb5be19c5504a2aa53d2f92b5df909a2417272b8c3e1d6c6f0c9f350b1d9.png) as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/example-425.json" "file_sha256" "efa73ad525176d966ea8e3d0cb3a6d25d43575a0d9c376bc1cb520aba762dde2"} {"question" "e-425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/3a73881568cbe0c10ae1e24446a47a522072eb67093c9d5fd34ca0862e02be7a.png)\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/4cf2e90080f1ae459e72ac253d6617d0defdcc3be58a1ca385bddc3215034eca.png)-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/321fc255901c5f86d2c324df57b60faa428e5cbdd2cba412f643cff6df19422e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/example-425.json" "file_sha256" "efa73ad525176d966ea8e3d0cb3a6d25d43575a0d9c376bc1cb520aba762dde2"} {"question" "e-426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/example-426.json" "file_sha256" "9ea947257b3c0e18a5fbf5b6ddbc00148722703f1b9e81c52544fae02582dddf"} {"question" "e-426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/f2a29faeb9db8e622a21a0e77bea911960a5c64d87a2c900ecd0c0213b2e4bce.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-8y{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-8(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-16 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $\\left|-\\dfrac{32}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{32}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/example-426.json" "file_sha256" "9ea947257b3c0e18a5fbf5b6ddbc00148722703f1b9e81c52544fae02582dddf"} {"question" "e-6797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/eb7118486df97c3abae6c8492170090df0c654064799e04826a39a94bcfa22a8.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/example-6797.json" "file_sha256" "d17af83b6b1e751481a2aa58cb3a835161906ecd7b8fe240ae4f1f872217c9ad"} {"question" "e-6797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $\\left|-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/example-6797.json" "file_sha256" "d17af83b6b1e751481a2aa58cb3a835161906ecd7b8fe240ae4f1f872217c9ad"}] "usage" {"e-425" [] "e-6797" [] "e-426" []} "basis" 1177 "retractions" [[17592186096358 :question/problem "Find the area bounded between the curve $x=y^2-2y+2$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=1$ and $y=2$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1041/Source/Images/ba7fd1150e24e1bce909556ef88947a8.png)"] [17592186096358 :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{c}^{d}x(y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2}(y^{2} - 2y + 2)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2}y^{2}dy - 2∫_{1}^{2}ydy + ∫_{1}^{2}2dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{1}^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & _{1}^{2} + 2y\\end{vmatrix}_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}) - (4 - 1) + (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3} - 3 + 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096359 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1041/Source/Images/0a73f14099d8a6940e2ee8a6785c25e2.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=2$. To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{c}^{d}x(y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}(y^{2} - 4)dy \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{2}(y^{2} - 4)dy \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{2}y^{2}dy - 8∫_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{3}}{3} \\mid _{0}^{2} - 8y \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(\\frac{8}{3} - 0) - 8(2 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{3} - 16 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{32}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\n\\left|-\\dfrac{32}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{32}{3}\n$$"] [17592186096476 :question/problem "Find the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1041/Source/Images/8eb4608fe3ade4f8a475b24df1952eb2.png)"] [17592186096476 :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{x + 1} \\\\\ny^{2} &= x + 1 \\\\\nx &= y^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{1}(y^{2} - 1)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}y^{2}dy - ∫_{0}^{1}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^{3}}{3} \\mid _{0}^{1} - y \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3} - 0) - (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\n\\left|-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
